{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a quiet storm of pink petals drifting over temple roofs. I spent mornings sipping matcha in hidden teahouses, listening to the rustle of bamboo groves and the distant chime of temple bells. No one rushed. No one shouted. Even the pigeons seemed to move with intention.\n\nOne day, I wandered Nishiki Market, where vendors offered pickled plums, fresh uni, and matcha mochi so soft it melted like cloud. I tried takoyaki for the first time—crispy outside, molten within—and asked the vendor, “Is this how you make it at home?” He grinned and said, “Yes. But my mother taught me to stir counterclockwise.” I didn’t know that mattered, but I believed him.\n\nIn Tokyo, I got lost in Shibuya’s scramble crossing, swept along by a tide of suits and sneakers. I didn’t panic. I just followed the rhythm. At a tiny izakaya tucked beneath a train line, I ate yakitori while an old man played shamisen in the corner. No one spoke English. No one needed to. We shared silence, sake, and smiles.\n\nI visited a Zen garden in Kamakura and sat for an hour, watching raked gravel ripple like water. A monk passed by, nodded, and left a single persimmon on the bench beside me. I didn’t eat it right away. I let it sit—like the moment, like the trip—until I was ready.\n\nJapan doesn’t demand your attention. It invites it.\n\nI left with no souvenirs except a folded origami crane in my pocket and a quiet understanding: beauty lives in stillness, in small gestures, in the space between breaths.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan.\n\nI remembered how to be still.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token